{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Triage: this is external-infra and needs an operator decision before any work starts.\nGrouping it with the deferred external-infra set (d353-b81c, e8d8-e2a1, 9018-3385,\nea66-45ea, 3057-4d76). It was not named in that set only because it was filed after\nthe set was drawn up.\n\n## Why it cannot be landed through this repo\n\nThe alarm is not managed here. `infra/terraform/` declares 20+\n`aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm` resources, all of them rebar's own infrastructure --\n`bridge_run_failures`, `mirror_out_of_sync`, `review_bot_merge_change_errors`,\n`replication_errors`, `voter_errors`, `gerrit_healthy_down`, `reviewbot_healthy_down`,\n`mcp_serving_path_down`, `bedrock_invoke_client_errors`, the autodeploy and disk\nalarms. A scan of `infra/**/*.tf` for `lmn-staging` returns zero hits, and\n`private-context-denied` appears nowhere in the tree outside this tracker's own\nevent log.\n\n`lmn-staging-private-context-denied` therefore lives in account `896586841071`,\ndeclared outside this repository. Every one of AC1-AC5 requires mutating live\nstaging monitoring in that account -- creating a log-based metric over S3 server\naccess logs and replacing a live alarm -- with no Gerrit change to review and no\n`Verified` run to prove it. That is the same shape as the other external-infra\ntickets being held.\n\n## AC6 satisfied: metric source\n\nRecording the metric source now, since it is established from the 46b2-fb3b\nevidence rather than from any change:\n\n    source   s3://lmn-staging-private-context-logs-896586841071/access/\n    format   S3 server access logs\n\nSelected fields and predicate:\n\n    http_status = 403\n    error_code  = AccessDenied\n    requester NOT LIKE arn:aws:sts::896586841071:assumed-role/*/lmn-staging-private-audit-*\n    requester  != arn:aws:iam::896586841071:user/joe_frontier\n    threshold   Sum > 5 over 300 seconds\n\nS3 server access logs are the only viable source: the account has no CloudTrail\ntrails and no event data stores, so S3 data-event denials are invisible to\nCloudTrail today. Adding a data-event trail is the alternative source, and it is a\ncost and retention decision rather than a technical one -- which is part of what\nneeds deciding.\n\nNote the source carries a delivery caveat that affects alarm design: S3 server\naccess logging is best-effort and delivered on a delay of minutes to hours, so an\nalarm built on it detects a denial burst well after it happens. A CloudTrail\ndata-event trail delivers in ~5-15 minutes and would give a tighter loop at higher\ncost. Whichever is chosen sets the achievable detection latency, so it should be\nchosen deliberately rather than inherited from the interim design above.\n\n## The decision\n\nAC1 and AC2 pull in opposite directions -- suppress the audit runner's expected\ndenials without blinding the alarm to a real unexpected denial -- and the design\nsketched in the description resolves that with a requester-exclusion predicate. The\nopen question is not how to write the predicate but which source to build it on and\nwho applies it, in an account this repo does not manage. Deferring to the operator.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789908725312790706,"uuid":"0a9e516a-39d6-4e14-bee6-b6fae3a11f3e"}